  'helper.q.hebrew': 'Do I need to know Hebrew?',
  /** HTML */
  'helper.a.hebrew': '<p>No — it is enough to recognize the letters. Every Hebrew word in a lesson comes with a translation and an explanation. If you don’t know the letters yet, open <a href="#/letters">“The letters in 5 minutes”</a>: a table of the 22 letters and 5 final forms with their names and numbers, and a short quiz.</p><p>In the “My Hebrew” menu, choose your level: “I don’t know the letters” — each letter shows its name, and word cards show how the word is read; “I know the letters” — the name appears on long press or hover; “I read Hebrew” — no hints. The level changes only the hints, not the puzzles or the points.</p>',
  'helper.q.count': 'How do I count gematria in my head?',
  'helper.a.count': '<p>Each letter is a number: <span class="he">א</span> to <span class="he">ט</span> are units (1–9), <span class="he">י</span> to <span class="he">צ</span> tens (10–90), <span class="he">ק</span> to <span class="he">ת</span> hundreds (100–400). So the letters are already sorted by place value: add the hundreds, the tens and the units separately, then join them left to right.</p><p>For example, <span class="he">שלום</span>: hundreds <span class="he">ש</span> = 300; tens <span class="he">ל</span> + <span class="he">ם</span> = 30 + 40 = 70; units <span class="he">ו</span> = 6. Total 300 + 70 + 6 = 376.</p>',
  'helper.q.turim': 'How do I read the Baal HaTurim?',
  'helper.a.turim': '<p>The commentary of the Baal HaTurim (Rabbi Yaakov ben Asher), printed in almost every Chumash, is made of short allusions. Its devices:</p><ul><li><b>Gematria</b> — two phrases with the same number are linked in meaning.</li><li><b>Rashei and sofei teivot</b> (<span class="he">ר״ת</span>, <span class="he">ס״ת</span>) — the first or last letters of neighboring words form a new word.</li><li><b>Notarikon</b> — each letter of a word is read as the start of a separate word.</li><li><b>“Two or three in the Masorah”</b> (<span class="he">ב׳ במסורה</span>) — a word appears in all of Tanakh exactly two or three times, and these places explain each other.</li></ul><p>Every number in the lessons is checked by code. Gematriot that do not add up in our spelling are left out of the lessons.</p>',
  'helper.q.methods': 'What other ways of counting are there?',
  'helper.a.methods': '<p>Besides the usual gematria (<i>mispar hechrechi</i>) there is <b>small gematria</b> (a letter’s value without zeros: <span class="he">י</span> = 1, <span class="he">ק</span> = 1), the <b>ordinal number</b> (<span class="he">א</span> = 1 … <span class="he">ת</span> = 22), the <b>great number</b> (final letters <span class="he">ך ם ן ף ץ</span> — 500…900), <b>full spelling</b> (every letter by its name: <span class="he">א</span> = <span class="he">אלף</span> = 111) and the <b>kolel</b> — when 1 is added for the word itself.</p><p>There are letter swaps too: <b>atbash</b> (<span class="he">א״ת ב״ש</span>) — the first letter of the alphabet swaps with the last, the second with the second to last; <b>albam</b> (<span class="he">א״ל ב״ם</span>) — the alphabet is split in two halves. You can try all these ways in the gematria calculator.</p>',
  'helper.q.stuck': 'I’m stuck on a riddle — what now?',
  'helper.a.stuck': '<p>Tap the word cards to see their letters and values. Break a word down here in “Gematria step by step”. If it still doesn’t work out, use the “Hint” button in the riddle: the answer stays yours but earns fewer points. Techniques for addition, subtraction, multiplication and division are in the “Mental math” section.</p>',
  'helper.q.finals': 'Why are final letters equal to regular ones?',
  'helper.a.finals': '<p>The games use standard gematria: final letters <span class="he">ך ם ן ף ץ</span> equal regular <span class="he">כ מ נ פ צ</span>. There is another system in which final letters count as 500–900, but our lessons don’t use it.</p>',
  'helper.q.names': 'Why is it written אלקים?',
  'helper.a.names': '<p>Out of respect for G-d’s Name, on screen and in print we write <span class="he">אלקים</span> instead of the full spelling, and the Four-Letter Name as <span class="he">ה׳</span>. Gematria is still computed from the real spelling.</p>',
  'helper.q.sources': 'Where do the primary sources come from and how do I read them?',
  'helper.a.sources': '<p>The Torah verses, Talmud, midrashim and commentaries a lesson rests on come from the open Sefaria library. After you solve a riddle, a “Primary sources” block opens under its lesson: the Hebrew original and a translation side by side, with a link to Sefaria.</p>',
  'helper.q.progress': 'Is my progress saved?',
  'helper.a.progress': '<p>Yes. Without an account — only on this device and in this browser: points, solved riddles and notes are stored locally, and on another device the lesson starts afresh. If you sign in (the account button 👤 at the top), your progress is kept in your account too — on all your devices.</p>',
  'helper.q.print': 'How do I print a lesson?',
  'helper.a.print': '<p>In a lesson, press “Print” (or pick it in the menu). You can include or hide the answers, the lesson text and the questions for yourself.</p>',
  'helper.q.contact': 'How do I suggest an idea or report a mistake?',
  'helper.a.contact': '<p>Write to us: <a href="mailto:Office@mychitas.app">Office@mychitas.app</a>. We read every message.</p>',

